Title:
ReflectivePluginLoader

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- ReflectivePluginLoader is a minimal Windows x64 reflective PE (DLL) mapper that loads a DLL directly from an in-memory byte buffer instead of using `LoadLibrary`.  
- It provides a hot-swappable plugin architecture via a clean `IPlugin` ABI, with a test harness that reads a DLL into memory, maps it, resolves exports, and calls `init/execute/cleanup`.  
- The loader implements core PE mapping steps (relocations, import resolution, section protections) and exposes a simple export-based interface (`create_plugin`, `destroy_plugin`, etc.) for module lifecycle management.  
- It’s useful for red team operators, offensive tool developers, and malware researchers building modular in-memory components or experimenting with reflective loading tradeoffs.  
- The repo is intentionally minimal and documents limitations (no TLS callbacks, delay-load, forwarded exports, CFG metadata, SEH registration), making it a good baseline for extending a custom mapper.

Technical Focus:
- Reflective DLL loading / in-memory PE mapping
- PE parsing: relocations, imports, section mapping & protections
- Export resolution and ABI design for plugins (`IPlugin`)
- Cross-module allocation safety (HeapAlloc + placement new)
- Modular/hot-swappable architecture for Windows payload components

Use Cases:
- Build modular in-memory “plugins” for a C2/agent without `LoadLibrary`
- Prototype reflective loaders and extend support for TLS/SEH/CFG as needed
- Create hot-swappable capability modules (e.g., command execution, discovery) loaded from bytes
- Research/detection testing around reflective mapping behaviors vs standard DLL loading
- Embed DLLs as byte arrays (via `file2hex.py`) for single-binary deployment

Keywords:
reflective loader, reflective DLL injection, PE mapper, in-memory loading, Windows x64, DLL, LoadLibrary bypass, import resolution, base relocations, section protections, export resolver, IPlugin ABI, HeapAlloc, placement new, CMake, harness.exe, MemoryModule, stephenfewer ReflectiveDLLInjection, TLS callbacks, SEH registration, CFG metadata